For UVa 10050 **"Hartals"**, here's a simple student-style C solution that works on OnlineGDB and looks natural for a competitive programming exam.

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main()
{
    int t;
    scanf("%d", &t);

    while(t--)
    {
        int n, p;
        scanf("%d", &n);
        scanf("%d", &p);

        int hartal[4000] = {0};

        for(int i = 0; i < p; i++)
        {
            int h;
            scanf("%d", &h);

            for(int day = h; day <= n; day += h)
            {
                if(day % 7 != 6 && day % 7 != 0)
                    hartal[day] = 1;
            }
        }

        int lost = 0;

        for(int day = 1; day <= n; day++)
        {
            if(hartal[day])
                lost++;
        }

        printf("%d\n", lost);
    }

    return 0;
}
```

### Sample Input

```text
2
14
3
3
4
8
100
4
12
15
25
40
```

### Sample Output

```text
5
15
```

### Logic

* Create an array to mark strike days.
* For each political party, mark every `h`-th day.
* Ignore Fridays and Saturdays:

  * Friday → `day % 7 == 6`
  * Saturday → `day % 7 == 0`
* Count all marked working days.

### Time Complexity

* **O(n × p)** in the worst case, which easily passes the UVa limits.

This is the kind of straightforward solution many students submit successfully for UVa 10050.
